 \section{Discussion}
 
-\subsection{Why TEE Doesn't Reduce Coupling}
+\subsection{Why the Current Artifacts Cannot Identify a TEE Effect}
 
-The core assumption behind CG+TEE was that multi-evaluator aggregation would strengthen the evaluator's directional signal, reducing both bias and noise. The data refute the bias-reduction component. The most plausible explanation is that GLM5.2's three independent calls are not independent in the relevant sense: they share the same model weights, the same training data, and the same position bias (win\_rate = 0.000 in prior work). Averaging three biased judgments produces a biased average.
+The core assumption behind CG+TEE was that multi-evaluator aggregation would strengthen the evaluator's directional signal, reducing both bias and noise. The current real-API archive cannot test that assumption: both confidence-gated arms collapse to uniform weights, so the recorded zero coupling is a property of the fallback path rather than a measured mitigation or null effect. A silent parse default can therefore masquerade as numerical success in one coordinate and failure in another.
 
-This is consistent with the TEE framework's own analysis~\cite{messing2026tee}: TEE reduces \textit{random} error (sampling variance across evaluator calls) but not \textit{systematic} error (evaluator bias). Coupling is driven by systematic evaluator preferences, not by random judgment noise. Similarly, SNR-based filtering~\cite{heineman2025snr} and prompt-sensitivity analysis~\cite{sclar2024quantifying} address variance but not bias---a broader pattern in LLM evaluation methodology. An alternative explanation---that the additive CG update rule itself prevents further $\gamma$ reduction regardless of evaluator quality---cannot be ruled out without testing TEE with a non-CG update rule.
+The TEE framework distinguishes random measurement error from systematic evaluator bias~\cite{messing2026tee}; that distinction remains a useful target for future work. SNR-based filtering~\cite{heineman2025snr} and prompt-sensitivity analysis~\cite{sclar2024quantifying} likewise motivate separating variance from bias. The present archive does not identify either component. Nor can it rule out an interaction between the additive CG update rule and evaluator quality.
 
 \subsection{What Would Close the Landscape}
 
-The results clarify what is needed to populate the empty favorable regime: an intervention that reduces evaluator \textit{bias} ($\gamma$), not evaluator \textit{noise} (CV). What the artifact supports is narrower than the caption this section replaces: confidence gating did not reduce bias here at all --- on these 15 seeds it raised $\gamma_{T\to V}$ by a factor of 4.3 --- while the one measured reduction of about 39\% belongs to evaluator \emph{calibration} (\texttt{cg\_tee\_final\_results.json}, uncalibrated vs calibrated $\gamma_{T\to V}$), which is listed below rather than claimed as a result of this run. Candidates include: evaluator calibration (correcting the evaluator's probability outputs before they enter TTRL), multi-model ensembles (using \textit{different} evaluator models, not just multiple calls to the same model), and baseline protocol redesign (replacing the fixed baseline with randomized alternatives to break position bias).
+The results clarify what a valid study would need: non-degenerate confidence parsing, model and endpoint identity in every result file, seed-level variance records, and an aggregation rule fixed before analysis. The simulation artifact shows a large direction reversal relative to the original hypothesis, but its CV values remain unreproducible. The 39.2\% scalar exists only in a separate \texttt{cg\_tee\_final\_results.json} comparison between its uncalibrated and calibrated blocks; that artifact has unresolved provenance and does not support either a CG or a TEE conclusion. Candidate interventions remain evaluator calibration, multi-model ensembles using \textit{different} evaluator models, and baseline redesign to break position bias.
 
 \subsection{Limitations}
 
-(1) Single executor/evaluator pair (DeepSeek-V4-Pro + GLM5.2). The finding that TEE does not reduce coupling may not generalize to evaluators with lower position bias. GLM5.2's extreme position bias (win\_rate = 0.000 in prior work) means its three calls are highly correlated; TEE may be more effective for evaluators with independent judgment noise. (2) $K{=}3$ is standard but the TEE benefit scales with $\sqrt{K}$ under independent errors; larger ensembles could show different behavior. (3) $N{=}15$ provides adequate power for $\gamma$ comparison (CIs distinguish standard from CG alone) but wider CIs for CG vs.\ CG+TEE. (4) The experiment does not test multi-model ensembles (different evaluator models), which may reduce bias where single-model ensembles cannot. (5) The coupling--noise tradeoff hypothesis remains unresolved---our null result rules out one plausible intervention but does not identify the solution.
+(1) The run notes name a single executor/evaluator pair, but the result files do not record or authenticate it. (2) $K{=}3$ is only the nominal design; no parser-valid real-API ensemble was observed, so no scaling claim can be made. (3) $N{=}15$ is the nominal archive size, not evidence of adequate power, because the confidence-gated real-API arms contain no valid outcome estimates. (4) The archive does not test multi-model ensembles with different evaluator models. (5) The coupling--noise tradeoff remains unresolved; this package does not establish a TEE null result.
 
 \section{Conclusion}
 
-We tested whether multi-evaluator ensembles (TEE) could be combined with confidence-gated TTRL (CG). The current package is a qualified reproducibility failure: simulation and real-API artifacts disagree, and the real run degenerates under parser fallback. The coupling--noise tradeoff therefore remains unresolved. A valid rerun must log parser success, model endpoint, per-seed outputs, and condition-level summaries before practical recommendations are made.
+We examined whether multi-evaluator ensembles (TEE) could be combined with confidence-gated TTRL (CG). The current package is a reproducibility failure: simulation and real-API artifacts disagree, the real run degenerates under parser fallback, and the CV values are not recoverable from per-seed records. The coupling--noise tradeoff therefore remains unresolved. A valid rerun must log parser success, model endpoint, per-seed outputs, and condition-level summaries before practical recommendations are made.
 
 \section*{Broader Impact Statement}
 
-This work provides a qualified negative result: multi-evaluator ensembles (TEE) reduce measurement noise but do not reduce evaluator bias in closed-loop LLM systems. The finding has direct implications for practitioners: to reduce evaluator preference coupling, invest in evaluator \textit{calibration} or \textit{multi-model} ensembles rather than repeated sampling of the same biased evaluator. The null result prevents wasted effort on a plausible but ineffective mitigation strategy. More broadly, the work contributes to a growing recognition that evaluator reliability (CV) and evaluator bias ($\gamma$) are distinct properties requiring distinct interventions---a distinction with implications for the design of LLM-based evaluation pipelines beyond the EPC framework.
+This work does not establish a TEE effect in either direction. It documents a reproducibility failure that prevents treating repeated evaluator sampling as evidence of bias reduction, noise reduction, or a null result. The practical implication is procedural: parser success, model identity, and per-seed outputs must be audited before a closed-loop evaluator experiment is interpreted. More broadly, the work illustrates why evaluator reliability (CV) and evaluator bias ($\gamma$) must be measured separately rather than inferred from a single aggregate.
 
 \section*{Reproducibility Statement}
-All experiments use official API endpoints with fixed model versions. Code and data released upon acceptance.
+The archived $N{=}15$ result files do not record model identity or parser-success rates. A corrected rerun must pin endpoint, model version, prompts, seeds, parser outcomes, and condition-level summaries, and retain the resulting per-seed records. Code and data remain under the provenance limits recorded in this paper.
 
